import{_ as n,P as i,d as a,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.07 Bounded Rationality, Emotion, Group Decisions, and Exploration","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.07 Bounded Rationality, Emotion, Group Decisions, and Exploration","xkChars":0,"xkWords":488,"xkLessons":{"D.07":{"title":"D.07 Bounded Rationality, Emotion, Group Decisions, and Probing","lead":"Finding more messages, asking more people, or waiting for another round of results does not always improve a decision. First examine whether the information is independent, whether it can change feasible actions in time, and the costs of waiting and obtaining it; also recognize that understanding can be valuable in itself.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4711–4726; source anchor section-d-07; short quotation translated from Chinese: “The value of knowledge need not be fully converted into action benefits, but decision-oriented investigation must compare delay and privacy costs.” The source excerpt for this topic is separately reproduced verbatim in source-slice.md; explanation and source text are kept separate.","Bounded rationality first concerns the information, time, attention, and execution resources people actually possess. Normative optimization asks how to choose under explicit goals and constraints; descriptive research asks how people actually choose; improvement recommendations ask what arrangements can be changed to help complete the task. These cannot be merged: divergence from a mathematical optimum may reflect different goals, information, or execution conditions, while describing a common choice does not establish that it should be retained.","Framing effects, reference points, beliefs, probability weighting, and utility curvature are distinct explanatory components, not items to collapse into “irrationality.” Given different reasonable priors, identical likelihood information can still yield different posteriors under Bayesian rules. Disagreement need not mean one party fails to understand reasoning. Judging a specific bias requires specifying the original problem, beliefs, presentation, losses, and available options, rather than diagnosing from a final choice that fails to match the analyst’s preferences.","Expert intuition can be useful in environments with learnable regularities and effective feedback; seniority and confidence alone do not guarantee accuracy. Where outcomes are delayed, feedback selective, or tasks frequently change, repeated experience may only reinforce errors. Emotion also cannot simply be treated as noise: it may express risks, values, and bodily states, or constrain the action set. Calmness does not prove correctness, and a strong reaction does not prove error; examine the relevant material and actual effects separately.","Memory, present bias, real future constraints, and execution fatigue should also remain separate. Failure to follow an earlier plan may reflect forgetting or time-varying preferences, but also new care responsibilities or physical loads; it cannot always be labeled poor willpower. Irrecoverable past investments differ from future relocation, care, contractual, and retaliation costs that remain payable. Recognizing real transfer costs is not synonymous with the sunk-cost fallacy; action comparisons must restore future costs to the analysis.","Group judgment involves four kinds of independence: independent answers, independent relationships, independent factual sources, and independent errors. Anonymous individual answers do not guarantee different sources; people without relational contact may still share the same erroneous data. Headcount cannot replace the conditions for collective intelligence. If multiple groups first produce one vote each and majority rule is then applied to representative votes, the outcome may oppose the majority of all individuals. Compressing group sizes and internal heterogeneity changes the aggregation target.","Choosing an aggregation method requires first stating the evaluation goal, representative eligibility, and weights at every level. One person one vote, equal weighting of groups, or protection of certain minority positions can be different normative choices; no calculation alone makes one inherently most reasonable. The majority counterexample below establishes only that aggregation does not commute. It does not decide how real institutions should vote or confer authority to represent others.","The evidentiary value of messages depends on underlying sources and selection into the record. Nine positive messages may merely repeat one underlying source, or may come from observations conditionally independent under both hypotheses. Nine news reports do not automatically justify multiplying likelihood ratios. At the same time, more supporters may change coordination capacity, available resources, and shared expectations even without new factual evidence. Evidentiary value and social-coordination value should be registered separately.","Information that is free, ignorable, and changes neither the objective nor the action set has nonnegative gross value under an optimal strategy. The reason is not that information is magically always correct, but that the old strategy remains selectable: completely ignoring the information at least preserves the original expected payoff, and reoptimization cannot do worse than a feasible old strategy. This conclusion depends on assumptions about information processing and choice capability. Costs, opportunities lost through waiting, privacy losses, changed goals, and misuse affect actual net value.","Small-step trials have positive decision value only when information can change an executable plan in time, risks are bearable, and stopping or alternatives are genuinely feasible. Smaller trials are not necessarily safer: repeated small steps may accumulate exposure, while waiting may miss a window. Knowledge can have value for understanding without all being converted to action payoffs. But if the aim is better decisions, compare relevant costs and stopping conditions for trials, delay, direct action, and inaction.","Eight topic entries cover Simon’s bounded rationality and satisficing; Kahneman and Tversky’s heuristics and prospect frameworks; expert intuition in natural settings; belief updating and motivation; emotion, risk, and the body; attention, memory, and time; group aggregation; and reversible choice and information value. They are leads for different tasks, not one unified conclusion that everyone is irrational. The strongest objection says every additional piece of information should be gathered; the nonnegative-gross-value theorem excludes actual acquisition costs. The opposite objection—that costs justify abandoning understanding—is also unsound: judge what concrete difference information can make and its independent epistemic value."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Normative optimum","leftText":"Seeking the best choice under disclosed goals and constraints.","rightTitle":"Actual choice and improvement","rightText":"Require behavioral materials and evidence of the effects of changed arrangements respectively."},{"leftTitle":"Independent responses","leftText":"Respondents did not coordinate while answering.","rightTitle":"Independent sources and errors","rightText":"They may still share underlying sources and common errors; check separately."},{"leftTitle":"Factual evidential value","leftText":"The incremental support a message provides for a hypothesis.","rightTitle":"Coordination value","rightText":"More supporters may change action opportunities without adding factual evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Gross information value","leftText":"The increment in optimal payoff when information is free and ignorable and other conditions remain unchanged.","rightTitle":"Actual net value","rightText":"Also includes fees, delay, privacy, opportunities, and misuse."},{"leftTitle":"Irrecoverable past investment","leftText":"The portion already paid that does not change with the current choice.","rightTitle":"Future transition costs","rightText":"Relocation, care, contracts, retaliation, and other costs still to be borne cannot be ignored as sunk."}],"boundaries":["Different posteriors from different priors do not automatically indicate irrationality; whether the priors themselves are grounded remains open to discussion.","Group size, independent responses, relational independence, source independence, and error independence cannot be conflated.","Nonnegative gross information value has strict conditions; it cannot be generalized to paid or unignorable information, changed goals, or lost opportunities.","Emotional intensity, seniority, and confidence do not directly establish accuracy; examples cannot diagnose real people.","All mathematical examples are bounded teaching constructions; there were no actual organizational votes, surveys, or experiments on people."],"questions":["Why can a majority of majorities oppose the individual majority?","What does the proof of nonnegative gross value for free, ignorable information depend on?","Why cannot the likelihood ratio be multiplied nine times for nine same-source messages?","Can strong emotion directly prove a judgment wrong?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-07/figures/section-d-07.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Hypothetical questions in original research: lives saved and deaths","href":"https://web.stanford.edu/~jlmcc/Presentations/tversky_kahneman_1981.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Hypothetical questions in original research: lives saved and deaths","text":"On page 453 of their original 1981 publication, Tversky and Kahneman report classroom questionnaires. Separate groups of 152 and 155 respondents faced disease-policy options with equivalent stipulated outcomes but different wording. In the group using lives saved, 72% chose the certain option; in the group using deaths, 22% chose it. The reported response summaries are actual paper materials, while the disease scenario is hypothetical and cannot be called a public-health disposition that really occurred.","sourceLabel":"Tversky & Kahneman, The Framing of Decisions and the Psychology of Choice, original journal scan p453","sourceUrl":"https://web.stanford.edu/~jlmcc/Presentations/tversky_kahneman_1981.pdf","sourceLocator":"Science 211, 1981-01-30, printed p453: classroom-questionnaire description; Problem1[N=152] and Problem2[N=155] and the immediately following discussion","boundary":"This lesson checks only that page’s questions and group summaries. It does not claim the same individuals changed choices, everyone behaves this way, or one unique psychological mechanism was identified; raw individual data were not recalculated and the experiment was not replicated.","href":"https://web.stanford.edu/~jlmcc/Presentations/tversky_kahneman_1981.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.07","start_line":4802,"end_line":4817,"startLine":4802,"endLine":4817}],"outputId":"section-d-07","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Specify whether the task is normative, descriptive, or improvement-oriented, and its information, time, attention, and execution constraints.","Distinguish beliefs, utility, probability weighting, and framing; one “irrationality” label cannot cover them all.","Check group-aggregation goals and four kinds of independence; trace underlying message sources and selection.","Compare gross information value under the same objective and action set, then add investigation, delay, privacy, and use costs.","Assign trials corresponding decision value only when feedback can timely change actually executable strategies and risk/stopping conditions hold."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: the same likelihood, different posteriors","text":"Let one positive message have likelihood ratio 3. A’s prior P(H)=1/5 gives prior odds 1/4, updated odds 3/4, and posterior 3/7. B’s prior is 4/5, odds 4, updated odds 12, and posterior 12/13. Both update by the same rule but reach different conclusions; disagreement alone cannot establish who is unable to reason.","assumptions":"Binary hypotheses, the same credible likelihood ratio for both people, and stipulated prior differences.","boundary":"Does not prove arbitrary priors reasonable or estimate probabilities for real events."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: reversal by a majority of majorities","text":"Take three groups of sizes 3, 3, and 9. Each of the first two casts 2 votes for and 1 against; the third casts 0 for and 9 against. By group-majority representatives, approval wins 2 to 1; among all individuals, there are 4 votes for and 11 against. Changing aggregation levels changes the result, so representation and weighting goals must be explicit.","assumptions":"All votes are constructed; no real election or political preferences.","boundary":"Does not judge which institution is normatively correct."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: nine retellings are not nine independent pieces of evidence","text":"Let the prior be 1/2 and each positive observation have likelihood ratio 3. If nine messages are deterministic repetitions of the same observation and retelling selection adds no information, combined odds remain 3 and posterior 3/4. If nine observations are conditionally independent under both H and non-H, odds are 3^9=19683 and posterior 19683/19684. The difference arises from source structure, not word count.","assumptions":"A fully explicit binary model and conditional-independence assumptions.","boundary":"Actual source independence requires verification; the high posterior is no promise of real reliability."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: free information has gross gains but may lose after payment","text":"Let state H have probability 1/2. Action A pays 10 in H and 0 otherwise; B pays 6 in both states. Without information, choose B for expected payoff 6. With perfect information, choose A in H and B otherwise, for expectation (10+6)/2=8 and gross gain 2. If investigation costs a fixed 3 and all else is unchanged, net payoff 5 falls below the no-investigation payoff 6, yielding net gain −1.","assumptions":"Utility units are teaching assumptions, with a risk-neutral expected-payoff criterion, perfect information, and unchanged actions.","boundary":"Does not reduce all human values to this utility or estimate returns to a real investigation."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Nine people each filled in agreement independently, so there are nine independent pieces of factual evidence.","effect":"Independent responses do not guarantee independent underlying sources and errors; information sources must be checked.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Information can always be ignored, so every real investigation is worthwhile.","effect":"Ignoring available information does not eliminate investigation fees, waiting, or privacy losses; gross and net values differ.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Those who remain commit a sunk-cost error, so relocation and care risks need not be considered.","effect":"Real costs still payable in the future are not irrecoverable past investment.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why can a majority of majorities oppose the individual majority?","answer":"Representative votes compress group size and within-group heterogeneity; the two aggregation goals and weights differ.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What does the proof of nonnegative gross value for free, ignorable information depend on?","answer":"The old strategy remains available, objectives/payoffs and action sets remain unchanged, and ignoring information is permitted under an optimal strategy.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why cannot the likelihood ratio be multiplied nine times for nine same-source messages?","answer":"They are not conditionally independent new observations; repeated content adds no corresponding evidence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can strong emotion directly prove a judgment wrong?","answer":"No. Examine its risk indications, values, and action effects; calmness is not proof of correctness either.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-07/figures/section-d-07.png","alt":"Decisions face resource constraints: information/time/attention and execution capability; distinguish beliefs and frames, as emotion and confidence do not determine truth; four kinds of group independence: responses/relations, sources/errors; state aggregation goals first, since group majorities ≠ individual majorities; distinguish gross and net information value, counting fees/delay/privacy, opportunities, and misuse; trials must change feasible strategies, involve bearable risks, and have genuinely feasible stopping or alternatives. Drawn by the author from D.07; Bayesian, voting, and payoff numbers are teaching constructions, not actual group data.","caption":"Drawn by the author from D.07; Bayesian, voting, and payoff numbers are teaching constructions, not actual group data."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Toggling underlying-source independence, aggregation by group or individual, and investigation fees could show changes in evidence or net value.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original questionnaire report supports a bounded framing comparison; fictional question scenarios and actual response summaries are strictly distinguished."},"openQuestions":["Not all original studies corresponding to 07.01–07.08 were obtained; the catalog cannot reconstruct every theoretical debate.","Only the questions and summaries on printed page 453 of the original study were checked, not raw response data, complete subsequent research, or within-person changes."],"taskId":"S-D-07","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 4625–4640; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["D.07: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"tversky-kahneman1981","title":"The Framing of Decisions and the Psychology of Choice","url":"https://web.stanford.edu/~jlmcc/Presentations/tversky_kahneman_1981.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Science211，453—458，1981-01-30","locator":"Printed p453: classroom-questionnaire description, Problem1[N=152] and Problem2[N=155], and the immediately following discussion","claim_support":["Sample sizes and proportions choosing the certain option for two differently worded questions; distinction between hypothetical problems and research summaries"],"limits":["Not a real public-health event; proves neither within-person reversal nor a single mechanism. Only this page was checked, with no replication conducted."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.07 Bounded rationality, emotion, group decisions, and trials; Chinese source lines 4711–4726; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-07/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-07/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-07/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-07/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"01070cc65a0d48f250e9df69b48d7d54d1010a09800bef4211d475756dab2efe","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-07.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-07.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-07.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,f){const t=i("SectionLesson");return a(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-07" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.07</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Bounded Rationality, Emotion, Group Decisions, and Exploration <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-07" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.07 Bounded Rationality, Emotion, Group Decisions, and Exploration {#section-d-07}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Finding more messages, asking more people, or waiting for another round of results does not always improve a decision. First examine whether the information is independent, whether it can change feasible actions in time, and the costs of waiting and obtaining it; also recognize that understanding can be valuable in itself.</span></div><p>In the main text, see §9, §11.5–11.8, and §12. Research entries cover 07.01–07.08.</p><p>Bounded rationality first asks what information, time, attention, and resources for execution are available when people decide. Determining what is normatively optimal, describing actual choice, and recommending improvements are three distinct tasks. Framing effects, reference points, beliefs, probability weighting, and utility curvature cannot be replaced by the label “irrational.” Given the same likelihood information, different priors can reasonably produce different posteriors.</p><p>Professional intuition can be useful in environments with learnable regularities and effective feedback. Seniority and confidence alone do not establish accuracy. Emotions can carry clues about risk and express values, and they can affect the actions available. Calm is no proof of being right, and an intense reaction is no proof of being wrong. Memory, present preferences, real future constraints, and fatigue from execution need to be distinguished.</p><p>Group judgment must distinguish independence of members’ responses, independence of relationships, independence of factual sources, and independence of errors. A majority of majorities can differ from the majority of all individuals. Several opinions can also repeat the same source. In a teaching example, each subgroup contributes one representative vote. This compresses away differences in group size and variation within the groups. To choose an aggregation rule, first state the evaluation objective and what qualifies someone to represent a group. “Collective wisdom” cannot substitute for a rule.</p><p>A small exploratory step has positive value only if information can change an executable plan in time, risk is bearable, and stopping or substitution is genuinely feasible. The value of knowledge need not all be converted into gains from action, but an investigation undertaken for a decision must weigh the costs of delay and privacy. Past investments that cannot be recovered differ from relocation, care, and contract costs and retaliation risks still to be faced. They must not all be treated as sunk-cost bias.</p><p>The evidential value of a message depends on the structure of its sources and the process by which it was included. The same nine positive reports can be repetitions of one underlying source or come from several conditionally independent observations. Their value for social coordination may still change as the number of supporters grows. Information that is free to obtain and can be ignored, and that leaves the objective and action set unchanged, has nonnegative gross value under an optimal policy. Investigation costs, lost opportunities, loss of privacy, and incorrect use enter the actual net value.</p><p>Research topics. 07.01 Bounded rationality and satisficing (Simon). 07.02 Heuristics, prospect theory, and framing effects (Kahneman and Tversky). 07.03 Naturalistic decision-making and professional intuition. 07.04 Belief updating, motivated reasoning, and cognitive dissonance. 07.05 Emotion, risk perception, and bodily states. 07.06 Attention, memory, and time preferences. 07.07 Group decision-making, groupthink, and collective wisdom. 07.08 Reversible choices, exploration, and the value of information.</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.07"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
